david chang is talking to us about his  struggles to overcome professional  and personal struggles that include much  more than the pandemic chang of course  is the celebrated chef  and restaurateur of 13 restaurants  around the world many  under the momofuku name he's also the  creator and host  of the dave chang show podcast and two  netflix shows including the food and  travel show  ugly delicious but in a deeply personal  new memoir  he talks about dealing with his  challenges and first on cbs this morning  he spoke to us  from his los angeles restaurant major  domo  it is a battle of survival that is as  clear as that  its survival is the word in this  industry what are we serving today  for chef david chang it's personal are  we doing pasta for two  the six time james beard award winner  has had to close  two of his restaurants and consolidate  two more  due to the pandemic you kind of like  being the underdog  i do because i get my juices flowing and  i get the best work done but this is  this is a very difficult obstacle  this crisis for the restaurant industry  comes as it's already confronting  me too allegations and it's notoriously  toxic work environment whether it's  workers rights benefits or abuse in a  kitchen  we need to have these conversations to  fix this  moving forward and i think before you  can sort of fix your culture and your  kitchen and your restaurants  you need to sort of work on yourself  first and  you know i've been a bully and i have  i have a temper and it's something i've  tried to address over the years  in his new memoir eda peach chang writes  i hate that the anger has become my  calling card  where did your temper come from the  funny thing is i don't think i really  had the temper  until i got into cooking i put so much  meaning in cooking  because it saved my life it gave me  purpose so  anyone that wound up not respecting it  or caring about something as much as i  did  meant that it was an attack on me and my  values  the child of north and south korean  immigrants  chang grew up in suburban virginia he  was a child  golf prodigy but always felt  uncomfortable  in school you didn't feel like you fit  in no i  i still have a hard time i've always  felt  you know in between in between what not  ever going to be part of white culture  and  never going to be part of korean  korean-american culture  but chang did feel at home in the  kitchen  his father disapproved of his career but  gave him a loan to launch his first  restaurant and that was his way of  expressing love  he was unable to say i love you chang's  idea  to open a japanese noodle shop in new  york city  in 2004 it was a huge gamble  you keep putting yourself in situations  where  part of you thinks you really could fail  you sound like my my psychiatrist too  it's like why do  i constantly put myself in situations  that are so precarious  every time i open a restaurant like  we're never doing this again and  you also say that opening a restaurant  is like heroin for you  it is cooking in general is like heroin  because you just get these dopamine hits  all the time this industry and how i do  my job  is 99 suffering in pain and misery  it's that one percent when things go  right that makes it all worthwhile  the beard award goes to david chang  momofuku was a breakout success but  chang had trouble  accepting the acclaim food and wine  called to  tell you that you were going to be one  of the best new chefs and you wanted to  decline  i did decline it and then dana cowan  said in 20 years of me doing this no  one's ever said no and you're not going  to be the first one  if you have low self-esteem and low  confidence and no one's ever told you  you're good  at anything and all of a sudden people  tell you you're good at something how  are you supposed to process that  the easiest way is to deflect it and say  it's not true and to put your head in  the sand and that's what i did  and i still do chang whose battle  depression  was close with food and travel icon  anthony bourdain  who died by suicide in 2018.  that must have really shaken you that  was hard  and still is hard when tony passed  um i think honestly that was one of the  reasons why  i decided to actually take this book on  in earnest  i was tired of being able to not talk  about this in public  chang now works with a therapist who  diagnosed him with bipolar disorder  i didn't realize that i was depressed in  high school or  or college until i actually got help  i just thought this is just me and  everything seems terrible and i'm sad  and i don't know why  i have these thoughts all i want people  to understand is  the hardest thing and the bravest thing  you can often do is just to say you need  help  his wife grace has been a big help  and now with son hugo to raise david  chang is trying to change  the future by being honest about his  past  being able to create a family with my  wife i'm learning there are more  important things than work  serious news what do you see when you  see hugo i want someone that  doesn't make the same mistakes i do i  just want to be the best father  i can my happiness is going to be the  systematic deprogramming of whoever the  hell  i think that i am and to be someone else  that i hope that i am  yeah what a great picture wow this book  is  all about david chang trying to  understand himself  and it's and it's fascinating because he  is just  blisteringly honest about everything i  mean he writes i feel a compulsion to  legitimize my depression  even now depression and the choice to  resist it are the only reasons you're  hearing from me here well i admire him  so much number one anthony that was a  great interview for david chang to open  up the way he did and people only do  that when they feel comfortable talking  to you so  bravo to you for that but when he said  the bravest thing you can do sometime  is to say you need help i remember  seeing he and grace out and about when  she was pregnant  and he was so happy about the baby  coming and hugo is adorable and  just imagine the kind of father he's now  going to be yeah oh i was a beautiful  piece  very touched by that and i i i'm sure he  wouldn't want me to say it but it seems  like he's becoming a beautiful person if  he never thought to himself  yeah i mean i think he's still wrestling  with all that stuff as you can see but  but but he but he's confronting it and  he does that in this book it's a great  read  david chang's new memoir eda peach is on  sale now wherever you buy your books  and hear more of our conversation with  david chang on today's cbs this morning  podcast
 

